findings.response_evidence:
GET /api/Feedbacks without any authentication token returned HTTP 200 with content-type application/json and a full array of feedback records, e.g.:
{"UserId":1,"id":1,"comment":"I love this shop! Best products in town! Highly recommended! (***in@juice-sh.op)","rating":5,...}
{"UserId":21,"id":4,"comment":"Please send me the juicy chatbot NFT in my wallet at /juicy-nft : \"purpose betray marriage blame crunch monitor spin slide donate sport lift clutch\" (***ereum@juice-sh.op)","rating":1,...}

The endpoint is reachable with zero authentication and discloses: (1) partial author email addresses embedded in the comment text (domain 'juice-sh.op' plus trailing local-part characters, e.g. '***in@juice-sh.op', '***ereum@juice-sh.op', '***der@juice-sh.op'), and (2) the numeric UserId foreign key directly associating each comment/rating with an internal user account identifier. This combination (partially unmasked email fragments + UserId linkage) returned to an unauthenticated caller constitutes disclosure of personally identifiable information without any access control (CWE-359), consistent with the known Juice Shop improper-PII-handling flaw on the public feedback API.
